From 6ff21717cf21963d777f6f4b998788fb93662313 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 02:36:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] fix: Alt$ reads a two-dimensional branch flat and an
 indexed one only where it points

The flat read was the mixed radix of the last dimension's levels, which is
ROOT's read only where the weak branch has one dimension. ROOT counts the
loop's index as one number over every level of it and reads the branch at
that number, into the elements its entry holds: a b[2][2] beside an
a[2][2][2] is read at i0 * 4 + i1 * 2 + i2, and an m[2][3] - Alt$(c, 0) of a
c[2][4] at the loop's six indexes rather than in rows of four. A dimension
given an index pins the run to what lies under it instead - Alt$(v[0], 0) is
one value an entry and Alt$(b[0], 0) one row - where the constant index being
0-d raised IndexError out of arrays() and Draw().

The read is done once, at the level the loop ends at, and not at all where
the branch went its own whole length down every level of the loop, since
there it read those elements flat as it went: an Alt$ draw keeps the speed it
had. A dimension given an index after one looped over is the one place this
still differs from ROOT, which repeats the rows its own strides name there,
and the module docstring says so.

Three tests carry ROOT's numbers for the shapes this decides - nested and
jagged weak branches, indexed ones, and a number an entry - and the older
test's prose named Alt$(v, 6) over a line drawing Alt$(v, 0).
